ntinuation-format.md`: §7.7.4 header/source-index seam, recovery format 2 exact shape, no snapshot body, fenced rehydration and actual lift checks; §7.11 backend/fragment wording; §7.11.1 canonical kernel and its two backends, continuation §4.6 pointer, clauses 1-5/10 on rows, stage letters and `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` citation.\n- This report holds the orchestrator-log text; `docs/orchestrator-log.md` was not edited.\n\n## Sources and verification\n\nRead `collab/phase7-plan.md` §3.1-3.9 and slices, `collab/7a-report.md` including D3, the timestamped 7b/7c reports, `collab/7c-review.md` and the 7d report. `collab/7a-review.md` is absent in this worktree. Checked the relevant implementations in `rows.cljc`, `rows/checkpoint.cljc`, `handoff.cljc`, `holder/export.cljc`, `holder/driver.cljc`, `compose.cljc`, and additionally `semantic_register/layout.cljc` for the backend pre-attach limit.\n\nVerification was foreground-only: reviewed the three document diffs against code, checked amendment markers and section references, checked balanced Markdown fences and authorized file scope, and ran `git diff --check`. No runtime tests were run for these documentation-only changes. No code, tests, pins, prompt, plan or orchestrator log was edited; no commit or remote action was made. Reported JVM results belong to 7d, not to this documentation slice. A non-Codex review remains the owner's next step.\n\n## Design defects: discrepancies between plan and code\n\n1. **7a D2 / 7c F3:** plan §1.1's id-counter variant cannot have an equal baseline after changing the root; the baseline includes `:yin.k/root`, so it is `:variant-conflict`. The equal-baseline alternative keeps the root and reverses row order. `decode` admits unsorted packs; one root/one pack holds for sorted `emit`, not every admitted encoding. The documents state code and the owner ruling; the plan is outside this slice's edit scope.\n2. **Recovery sketch:** plan §3.2 includes a redundant `:yin.k/descriptors` set and passes `:header` to raw fenced lower. Format 2 has only tag/version, pack address/root/base64 pack, local header and local state. `rehydrate-fenced` checks header/state before `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}`; attached pack descriptors rebuild the retained served table. No second snapshot body or compatibility reader exists.\n3. **Custody staging:** plan §3.1 says four stages over the tree. Code runs stores, pendings/install records and dependencies as three passes, then `check-custody!` once for the tree. All precede attachment. Grammar resides in `rows/custody-baseline`; the rows inspector wraps it, avoiding a namespace cycle.\n4. **Lift ordering (7a D4):** serve operations happen during the walk before exhaustion/protection/custody refusal. The holder retains successful serves and reuses them on retry; no zero-serve promise was added.\n5. **7d source-index seam:** the plan's slice says holder-seams supplies source-index. Code requires a function on `compose/open!` and forbids holder override; compose forwards it to the driver. Trees are associations, not a new identity or resolver. A alone identifies code; source-index/fetch/admission keeps the AST retrievable.\n6. **Install verification boundary:** Q3 is implemented through response self-consistency: non-nil parent/link-id equality, manifest-named derivation hash, tree input and image-A output, plus image admission. Code does not add an independent claimed-image-address field check or fetch/admission path; composition-known address binding stays with the linker. The stronger old prose is identified as intended contract and the actual checks are stated.\n7. **Retained backends:** old plan/revision rationale said the de Bruijn kernels die at phase 8. The binding owner ruling keeps them as backends, without widening the fragment. `layout/lower-defect` checks image collision, installs, free-env procedures and register payload primitives before attach; it does not implement a universal fragment-membership gate. In particular, retained-FFI row 4 is excluded by the harness, not rejected there before attach. UCF states refuse-the-remainder as the owner's requirement, and continuation §4.6 states this code limit rather than claiming the missing gate exists. 6c Q2 remains open.\n8. **Acceptance inventory/evidence:** the plan's Stage E inventory says 12 tests in each suite; 7d reports 13 each. Its JVM suites and clean JVM/JVM process gate do not prove Node/Dart or killed four-pair JVM/Node acceptance. No new production defect was established by this documentation work.\n\n## Open questions\n\n6b Q1/Q10 (install wire phase/parked child), Q2 (recomputed census), Q5 (registry re-discharge), Q6 (live-code conservatism), Q7 (primitive shadowing), Q8 (preflight cost), Q9 (non-fresh receiver), and Q4's broader correlation agreement remain open. 6c Q2/Q3 retain their open status; 6c Q1's accepted 61/350 matrix remains normative. The row-4 backend refusal gap above is evidence for Q2, not an implemented seam or fragment expansion. REPL custody is an accepted gap until phase 8. Tri-host Stage E, killed process acceptance and the non-Codex documentation review remain owner/orchestrator work.\n\n## Orchestrator-log text (10 lines; owner to paste)\n\n```text\n2026-10-14 — Slice 7e documents the phase-7 row carrier against code and 7a-7d reports.\nOnly continuation, transport-tuples and UCF designs changed; orchestrator log was left to its owner.\nCustody is nil for every fork row; exclusive roots use the five-key slot by kind.\nLift header/protection order and retained serves are stated as implemented.\nLower runs three tree passes, custody once, then attachment; Q3 install checks are documented.\nFenced recovery keeps all tasks exporting, exact counters and detached waits/parked records.\nRecovery format 2 stores one pack plus local header/state, before fencing both stored objects.\nPack BLAKE3 address is storage identity; root is in baseline, so counter variants conflict.\nBackends remain after phase 8 on their fragment; correlation and backend refusal gaps stay visible.\nDocs checks pass; non-Codex review and the remaining Stage E host/process gates are still required.\n```\n\nREADY\n       8 collab/1792530000000-engineer-7e-docs.prompt.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013rFoShqsNTr86nRoBEFf97","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"subcommandResults"}}]}
