ow.\n27\t-**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §5.2 B1, §7.2):** the\n28\t-merged lower (`rows/check-install!`) verifies, before any attachment:\n29\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7b enforces install link and manifest/image agreement at lower.\n30\t+The merged lower (`rows/check-install!`) verifies, before any attachment:\n31\t the phase is one of `#{:running :parked}` (`:install-phase`); the\n32\t response is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` whose\n33\t `:yin.module/name` is the row's module and whose `:yin.module/exports`\n34\t@@ -401,9 +402,16 @@ receiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`); and the carried\n35\t child task's kind is `:blocked` (`:install-child`), the only kind the\n36\t engine retains at a safepoint (a halted or parked child is linked in the\n37\t sweep it halts in, so phase `:running` is a wire phase the codec accepts\n38\t-but the engine never exposes at a safepoint). The link-id-equals-parent\n39\t-and image-address-equals-manifest checks of the paragraph above are\n40\t-**not** implemented (continuation §11 Q3).\n41\t+but the engine never exposes at a safepoint). Lower also requires\n42\t+non-nil `parent` equal to response `:yin.link/id` (`:install-link-id`).\n43\t+The response's `:derivation` record must hash to the manifest's\n44\t+`[:yin.module/derivations :yin.semantic/code]` address; its\n45\t+`:yin.ledger/input` must equal `:yin.module/tree` and its output must\n46\t+hash-identify the image's `:value` vector (`:install-image`). These are\n47\t+response self-consistency checks; the linker's source-index, fetch and\n48\t+admission path binds code and trees to composition-known addresses.\n49\t+A remains the only code identity and the Universal AST is never lost.\n50\t+No new resolver or independent claimed-image-address check is added.\n51\t \n52\t ### 5.5 The root\n53\t \n54\t@@ -440,25 +448,27 @@ neither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\n55\t never collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\n56\t ordered waits.\n57\t \n58\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner chose nil forks and phase 7 restricts custody keys.\n59\t `custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n60\t §7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n61\t \n62\t | role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n63\t |---|---|---|---|\n64\t | root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n65\t-| root | blocked / parked | fork | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` |\n66\t+| root | blocked / parked | fork | nil |\n67\t | root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n68\t | root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n69\t | child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n70\t \n71\t-The explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\n72\t-empty-header fork representation: a fork root proposes nothing and is\n73\t-never upgraded to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n74\t-`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\n75\t-unchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n76\t+Fork custody is nil in every row; it proposes nothing and is never\n77\t+upgraded to custody. An exclusive-only receiver refuses a fork as\n78\t+`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. The slot admits only `:yin.k/policy`,\n79\t+`:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/arbitration`, `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and\n80\t+`:yin.k/origin`. No current lease, epoch or holder enters a body;\n81\t+origin's predecessor lease is lineage evidence.\n82\t \n83\t The root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\n84\t-address**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n85\t+semantic identity**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n86\t are inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\n87\t plain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\n88\t keeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\n89\t@@ -687,22 +697,23 @@ reference per cursor cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n90\t \n91\t ## 7. Equality and the content address\n92\t \n93\t-The transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\n94\t-the root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cursor cell\n95\t-id through the references, and to every cursor cell's contents through the\n96\t-census. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\n97\t-and under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\n98\t-renaming of cursor-cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\n99\t-the row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\n100\t-the pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\n101\t-Jing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\n102\t-supplies the order within each body and the numbering of cursor cells. Jing is\n103\t-asked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n104\t-\n105\t-Occurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\n106\t-the body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\n107\t-occurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\n108\t-occurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n109\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: content stores verify the whole pack's bytes, not the root row's hash.\n110\t+The transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. The root id\n111\t+commits to reachable content and cursor-cell contents through the census;\n112\t+equal root ids express semantic equality under §6's canonical numbering.\n113\t+The **pack address** is BLAKE3 of the canonical CBOR bytes of the whole\n114\t+pack. It is the content-store key, ledger `:yin.k/id`, grant checkpoint,\n115\t+offer variant id and report `:yin.k/result`; it differs from the root id.\n116\t+The baseline records the root as `:yin.k/root`.\n117\t+\n118\t+`emit` sorts `:yin.k/rows` by the string of each row id, so canonical\n119\t+emission yields one pack for one root on each host. `decode` still\n120\t+accepts other row orders. Reversing rows therefore gives another pack\n121\t+address with the same root and baseline; re-emitting an id-counter change\n122\t+changes the root and is `:variant-conflict` (7a D2). Occurrence and origin\n123\t+remain inside the root's hash. The holder stores the whole pack as one\n124\t+object, after recovery format 2 and before journaling the fence; it does\n125\t+not store rows separately.\n126\t \n127\t ## 8. Validation on lower\n128\t \n129\t@@ -800,15 +811,15 @@ one attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n130\t     operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n131\t     never adds missing header keys.\n132\t \n133\t-**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §7.2).** The merged\n134\t-`rows/lower-task` runs steps 8-10 as four stages, **each over the whole\n135\t-task tree before the next, and all before the first attachment** (step\n136\t-11): (a) stores: no reserved name keys the root store or a module store\n137\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phase 7 runs custody once after three per-tree stages.\n138\t+`rows/lower-task` runs stores, pendings/install records and dependencies\n139\t+**each over the whole task tree before the next**, then custody once for\n140\t+the tree, all before the first attachment (step 11): (a) stores: no reserved name keys the root store or a module store\n141\t (`:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`); (b) pendings and install\n142\t records: role, every `:install` pending names a carried install, kind\n143\t consistency, parked id, every install record as §5.4 as amended\n144\t (`:role :incomplete-install :kind :parked-id :install-phase\n145\t-:install-response :install-child`); (c) dependencies: `requires`'\n146\t+:install-response :install-link-id :install-image :install-child`); (c) dependencies: `requires`'\n147\t `:yin.k/segments` equals `code`'s key set (`:requires`), every segment\n148\t has a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry, every `store-of` of this task's\n149\t closures and environments names one of **this task's** module-store\n150\t@@ -816,10 +827,15 @@ rows (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/stores …}`), every\n151\t vector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`:segment`), every\n152\t closure row names a closure tuple of its vector with the same binders\n153\t (`:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`), every `:prim` is the receiver's\n154\t-(`:yin.k/unsatisfied`); (d) custody: nil only (`:yin.k/unsupported\n155\t-:custody`). Of step 8's text, the op-id rules and the correlation\n156\t-agreement are not implemented (fork-only custody has no `next-op-seq`;\n157\t-continuation §11 Q4); of step 9's, the census is not recomputed from the\n158\t+(`:yin.k/unsatisfied`); (d) custody: `custody-baseline` validates the\n159\t+slot and op-ids across the tree, refusing fork ids, missing origin,\n160\t+self-occurrence, out-of-range sequences and duplicates. A fork under\n161\t+`:exclusive` refuses; halted exclusive needs no grant; blocked/parked\n162\t+exclusive checks grant evidence, the pack checkpoint, binding, tenure,\n163\t+input prefix and protection/enrollment in both directions before attach.\n164\t+`:fenced` skips grant/protection and restores exporting state (§9).\n165\t+The broader correlation agreement remains unimplemented\n166\t+(continuation §11 Q4); of step 9's, the census is not recomputed from the\n167\t rows (continuation §11 Q2), and `requires` beyond segments, trees and\n168\t primitives (modules, streams, cursor profiles) is not evaluated on\n169\t lower; free names are discharged at lift only (continuation §7.1).\n170\t@@ -856,12 +872,32 @@ environment, the walk (which fixes the segments, contexts and module\n171\t stores), code, the per-context dependency closure over live code, tree\n172\t coverage from the composition's `:source-index`, frame validation of\n173\t the assembled task, then canonical numbering and emit; the slice is the\n174\t-whole root store and every parked record; custody is nil. The nested carrier's value table\n175\t+whole root store and every parked record.\n176\t+\n177\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phase 7 adds the header and custody checks after frame validation.\n178\t+`:header` nil means fork; otherwise enrollment remains local and the root\n179\t+slot is written by kind after exhaustion/protection checks. The lift then\n180\t+runs `custody-baseline` for the tree and emits sorted rows (§7). It can\n181\t+serve streams during the walk before a custody refusal; the holder\n182\t+retains successful answers for retries. The nested carrier's value table\n183\t (`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cursor cell\n184\t table becomes the census.\n185\t \n186\t **Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n187\t \n188\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: format-2 recovery uses an administrative fenced lower.\n189\t+`rows/lower-task {:fenced true}` runs the carrier checks and custody\n190\t+grammar before attach, skips grant/protection, then gates every task\n191\t+`:exporting`. Waits and all parked records move to\n192\t+`:yin.k/recovery-waits` and `:yin.k/recovery-parked`, outside the scheduler;\n193\t+the id counter is the carried value exactly and\n194\t+`:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` retains resource-key/cursor-cell associations.\n195\t+Child spawning disables stream construction and initializes nothing.\n196\t+The recovery reader checks its local header and `:yin.k/state` against\n197\t+the pack before lower, extracts the export record, and requires identical\n198\t+re-encoded pack bytes. It restores no tenure or runnable gate and refuses\n199\t+abort after restart. Ordinary resume still adopts `max(local, carried)`.\n200\t+\n201\t The laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n202\t \n203\t - **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n204\tdiff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\n205\tindex 4b575812..c15025bb 100644\n206\t--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\n207\t+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\n208\t@@ -1920,10 +1920,11 @@ kind `:reason-mismatch`, with the holding task's path; the lift\n209\t refuses the same holds on its own behalf. A task is not at a liftable\n210\t safepoint while it holds any of these.\n211\t \n212\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the holder now exports rows with a local header and source index.\n213\t The lift's custody input is one **header** argument -- supplied to\n214\t-`holder.export/prepare [machine record serve! header]`, stored in the\n215\t-export record as `:header`, and passed to `export-task` through its\n216\t-`:header` option -- `nil` or:\n217\t+`holder.export/prepare [machine record serve! header source-index]`,\n218\t+stored as `:header`, and passed to `rows/lift-task` through its `:header`\n219\t+option -- `nil` or:\n220\t \n221\t     {:yin.k/occurrence  O\n222\t      :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity i\n223\t@@ -1934,39 +1935,51 @@ export record as `:header`, and passed to `export-task` through its\n224\t      :yin.k/next-op-seq n\n225\t      :yin.k/enrolled    #{stream-identity ...}}\n226\t \n227\t-A `nil` header lifts today's version-0 fork; a header lifts version 1,\n228\t-exclusive, in the canonical codec, with `:yin.k/policy\n229\t-:yin.k/exclusive` added by the lift. The occurrence is minted once by\n230\t-the driver and persisted before prepare is called; `:yin.k/enrolled`\n231\t-is the composition's declaration from the ledger reader; it is never\n232\t-encoded into the UCF body, but the header stays in the retained export\n233\t-record. The live record can contain authentic closures and is not itself\n234\t+A nil header lifts a version-3 fork with nil custody; a header lifts an\n235\t+exclusive root with custody by kind (continuation §2.6). The driver mints\n236\t+and persists the occurrence before prepare. Enrollment stays local,\n237\t+never in the pack. The source index is the composition/linker's\n238\t+`{A #{tree-address}}`, supplied by `compose/open!` as a function and\n239\t+read by the driver; holder configs cannot override it. Code and trees\n240\t+load through the linker's source-index, fetch and admission; A is the\n241\t+only code identity and the Universal AST remains retrievable.\n242\t+\n243\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: export recovery is format 2, one pack plus holder-local state.\n244\t+The live export record can contain authentic closures and is not itself\n245\t a canonical data object. `holder.export/freeze [machine prepared-record]`\n246\t-encodes a separate content-addressed export-recovery object, tagged\n247\t-`:yin.k/export-recovery` with format version 1, using the handoff value/cursor-cell\n248\t-codec. It returns recovery bytes/address beside the exact handoff body\n249\t-bytes/address; both are stored durably before the journal's fenced event.\n250\t-Recovery retains the local enrolled declaration, prior gate, ordered waits,\n251\t-parked continuations, issue stamps, descriptors and dependency closure.\n252\t-The enrolled declaration still never travels in a published handoff body.\n253\t-\n254\t-`holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` validates the recovery object and its\n255\t-embedded body before administrative attachment. It creates authentic\n256\t-fresh-receiver-owned values and preserves resource aliases and distinct\n257\t-cursor cells without program reads, cursor minting, writes, closes, initialization\n258\t-or execution. Every task stays exporting, with waits outside scheduler\n259\t-queues. The prior gate is retained only as data; recovery is neither grant\n260\t-admission nor permission to abort back into execution. Runtime composition\n261\t-functions and handles are the environment, not the canonical journal.\n262\t-Incomplete preparations cannot manufacture a missing snapshot on restart.\n263\t-Recovery also retains a complete codec snapshot when legacy fork bytes\n264\t-omit transitive dependencies; the published version-0 bytes stay unchanged.\n265\t-For version 1, the module-store fixed point is a correctness repair:\n266\t-canonical ordering uses a separate scratch census and did not populate\n267\t-the emitted dependency table. Bodies that previously omitted transitive\n268\t-module stores now contain them and have different content addresses; this\n269\t-is not a new handoff version or closure format. The transitive-store test\n270\t-pins the old one-pass body and corrected body bytes by their addresses.\n271\t+emits this separate content-addressed object:\n272\t+\n273\t+```clojure\n274\t+{:yin.k/export-recovery true\n275\t+ :yin.k/version 2\n276\t+ :yin.k/pack-address a\n277\t+ :yin.k/root B\n278\t+ :yin.k/pack-bytes base64\n279\t+ :yin.k/header h\n280\t+ :yin.k/state {:gate g :origins n :issued n :issues [..]\n281\t+               :children {module-name state}}}\n282\t+```\n283\t+\n284\t+Here a is BLAKE3 of the published pack bytes and B is its structural\n285\t+root id. The recovery object's address hashes its own canonical bytes.\n286\t+The header is nil for fork and retains the local enrolled declaration for\n287\t+exclusive; state retains each task's prior gate, origin/issued counters,\n288\t+ordered wait issue stamps and recursive child metadata. The pack already\n289\t+carries the whole store, every parked record and reachable module stores.\n290\t+There is no second snapshot body or duplicate descriptor set.\n291\t+\n292\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: fenced row lowering reconstructs data without admitting execution.\n293\t+`holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` checks format, recovery address,\n294\t+pack address/grammar, root, header/custody agreement and state/tree shape\n295\t+before attachment. It calls `rows/lower-task` with `:fenced true`,\n296\t+attaches once per stream identity, and creates fresh receiver-owned values\n297\t+with cursor-cell aliases and distinct cursor cells preserved. Every task\n298\t+stays exporting; waits and parked records are outside the scheduler and\n299\t+move into the retained export record. Counters are exact; child spawning\n300\t+initializes nothing and performs no program IO. Re-preparation uses\n301\t+attached descriptors and requires byte-identical pack re-emission. No\n302\t+tenure, custody permission or runnable gate is recovered; abort after\n303\t+restart refuses. Composition functions and handles remain environment.\n304\t \n305\t The driver's exit persists recovery and body, journals the fence, attempts\n306\t the stable successor offer, reports that exact successor/body, carries\n307\t@@ -1992,24 +2005,20 @@ release opens with the next step, after the terminal record.\n308\t Reopened holders do not regain tenure from persisted clock readings:\n309\t they reconcile, release and re-enter candidacy, never resume execution.\n310\t \n311\t-Kind and\n312\t-header agree at lift: blocked and parked roots carry occurrence,\n313\t-arbitration, counter and policy, and origin when present; a halted\n314\t-root carries origin only, and a header-bearing halt without an origin\n315\t-is refused -- it is not automatically downgraded (a halt with no\n316\t-origin at all is a version-0 result under a `nil` header); install\n317\t-children carry no header. Before answering `:ok`, a version-1 root\n318\t-lift runs `checkpoint/inspect` on its own bytes and address, then\n319\t-`handoff/validate-body` on the decoded body; install children do not\n320\t-independently undergo the root custody inspection. Any refusal\n321\t-returns as the lift's refusal and nothing is published. A lift under a\n322\t-header refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:op-seq-exhausted`, at the\n323\t-2^52-1 counter of a blocked or parked root (a halted root ignores that\n324\t-header value), kind `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`,\n325\t-`:ffi-request` or `:link-request` entry, in the root or an install\n326\t-child, with no operation id whose target identity is enrolled, and\n327\t-`:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an operation id on an\n328\t-unenrolled target.\n329\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: custody is checked on the abstract row task, not by re-reading legacy bytes.\n330\t+Kind and header agree at lift: blocked/parked exclusive roots carry\n331\t+policy, occurrence, arbitration, counter and optional origin; halted\n332\t+exclusive roots carry origin only and a header-bearing halt without\n333\t+origin refuses. Forks and all children carry nil. After walking and frame\n334\t+validation, `rows/lift-task` checks exhaustion at 2^52-1 on a\n335\t+blocked/parked exclusive root and protection across all retained\n336\t+put/FFI-request/link-request waits, then runs `custody-baseline` before\n337\t+emit. Enrolled writes without an id refuse `:unprotected-pending`; an id\n338\t+on an unenrolled target refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream.\n339\t+Fork ids refuse. Successful serves may precede these refusals; the holder\n340\t+retains them for retry. The authority uses `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`,\n341\t+whose baseline includes the root id. No current lease, epoch or holder\n342\t+enters a body; origin carries predecessor lineage.\n343\t \n344\t Abort is permitted only before any possibly accepted offer attempt, or\n345\t upon authoritative evidence that this occurrence has never been\n346\t@@ -2890,7 +2899,12 @@ the review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\n347\t   to row sets with equal root ids after the harness's specified\n348\t   re-emission (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §5.5) from\n349\t   the semantic-register kernel and from the stack and register kernels\n350\t-  through their layouts, the stack kernel with its tail-completion seam;\n351\t+  through their layouts, the stack kernel with its tail-completion seam.\n352\t+  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner retains both kernels as backends on a fixed fragment.\n353\t+  The stack and register kernels are backends of the semantic-register\n354\t+  VM, checked on their documented fragment and required to refuse the\n355\t+  unsupported remainder before attach (continuation design §4.6). They are retained\n356\t+  after phase 8 without widening the fragment;\n357\t   and the §7.3.4 image-equality assertion (the two AST projection lanes\n358\t   and the direct vector load yield one image and address).\n359\t - **Exporting-state integration.** The lift driver's exporting transition\n360\t@@ -3052,9 +3066,11 @@ claim that all five blockers have closed.\n361\t   item 12). Exercise distinct activation depths and captured environments\n362\t   at one pc, a nonempty ready queue, a nonsafepoint pc, and a kept response\n363\t   cursor shared by two waiters. Omit the portable cursor profile for the\n364\t-  refusal case. Separately prepare the three engines intended to\n365\t-  reconstruct canonical frames (the semantic-register kernel; the\n366\t-  register kernel; the stack kernel after its tail-completion seam) with\n367\t+  refusal case.\n368\t+  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: cross-engine conformance covers the documented backend fragment.\n369\t+  Separately prepare the canonical kernel and its two backends\n370\t+  (semantic-register; register; stack after its tail-completion seam),\n371\t+  within continuation design §4.6's fragment, with\n372\t   a nontrivial call stack, a captured closure, and a blocked effect at\n373\t   the handoff point.\n374\t - Action and expected outcome: On each host, lift a parked task and lower\n375\t@@ -3201,12 +3217,19 @@ they are not moved here and nothing below relies on them as custody\n376\t evidence. The ownership row keeps its own contract; the rows below\n377\t are the wire-level clauses that row's tests must also exercise.\n378\t \n379\t-- Invariant: A version-1 body, envelope, binding, and admission\n380\t+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phase 7 re-bases custody evidence on row packs without codec dispatch.\n381\t+The clauses below now use version-3 packs and the inspector\n382\t+`yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`. Stage C is authority/admission (7a),\n383\t+Stage D is holder/composition (7b/7c), and Stage E is the scenario/process\n384\t+matrix (7d). Direct versions 0/1/2 codec tests remain only until phase 8;\n385\t+the holder and authority have no compatibility reader or version dispatch.\n386\t+\n387\t+- Invariant: A version-3 pack, envelope, binding, and admission\n388\t   outcome mean the same on JVM, Node, and Dart, and every breach of\n389\t   their grammar is a data outcome before a machine is restored or an\n390\t   effect is committed.\n391\t-- What M-next B claims: the grammar is published. It lands no code,\n392\t-  passes no test, and closes no row. `handoff-version` is still 0.\n393\t+- Historical M-next B claim: publication of grammar alone closed no\n394\t+  runtime row. Phase-7 gates below require row-carrier evidence.\n395\t - How each clause is tested on three hosts: by canonical byte\n396\t   fixtures decoded on each host, so a rule about integer kind or key\n397\t   presence is checked on the bytes and not on a host number type;\n398\t@@ -3216,53 +3239,49 @@ are the wire-level clauses that row's tests must also exercise.\n399\t Clauses, with the stage that owes the evidence\n400\t (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3):\n401\t \n402\t-1. Version gate (7.2.1). Fixtures: version 2, absent, a float `1.0`,\n403\t-   a version-0 child in a version-1 root. Assert\n404\t-   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` with the found and supported versions,\n405\t-   or `:yin.k/undecodable` for the mixed tree, with zero attach\n406\t-   calls, zero proposals, and no machine: recursive validation has\n407\t-   no side effect. The integral float is refused on Node as on the\n408\t-   others. A version-0-only reader refuses a version-1 body. A\n409\t-   version-0 body still lowers as a fork on a reader that speaks\n410\t-   both; a composition requiring exclusive refuses it. Stage: D.\n411\t-2. Custody header (7.2.1). Fixtures omit each required key, give a\n412\t-   fork policy, a nil occurrence, an origin equal to the body's\n413\t-   occurrence, and header keys other than the origin on a halted\n414\t-   root. Root and embedded child are distinguished: any header key\n415\t-   on a child, a halted child with an origin included, is refused,\n416\t-   and a halted child without one validates. Assert\n417\t-   `:yin.k/undecodable` with the path. Stage: D.\n418\t-3. Sequence state (7.7.8). Assign-before-append, one increment,\n419\t-   retention across `full`, unknown effect, and `:suspended`; the\n420\t-   counter restored exactly; children drawing from the root.\n421\t-   Stage: D for assignment and restoration; C for durable input\n422\t-   replay; E for crash and regrant through the composition.\n423\t-4. Carried ids (7.4.3, 7.7.8). An id crosses park, bytes, and lower\n424\t-   unchanged on `:put`, `:ffi-request`, and `:link-request`, and the\n425\t-   retry is fenced. Fixtures: an id on `:next`, a sequence at or\n426\t-   above the counter, a duplicate id, an id in a body without an\n427\t-   origin, an id naming the body's own occurrence. Assert\n428\t-   `:yin.k/undecodable`. Protection mismatch in each direction is\n429\t-   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream; a first exclusive export\n430\t-   over an attempted write is `:unprotected-pending`. Stage: D for\n431\t-   these structural checks. Stage: C for authoritative membership\n432\t-   and ancestry: an ancestor id the granted checkpoint never\n433\t-   carried is refused, a carried one is admitted, a child's carried\n434\t-   id counts, and an orphan successor gives no ancestry.\n435\t-5. Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3), both versions.\n436\t-   A `:park` or `:call-effect` reason and an `:install` pending\n437\t-   without its entry refuse at decode; a `:parked` body's frames\n438\t-   restore in order; a response that fails verification and a phase\n439\t-   outside `:running` and `:parked` refuse; each of the two phases\n440\t-   crosses with each child kind `:blocked`, `:parked` and `:halted`,\n441\t-   a `:running` phase over a blocked child and an explicitly parked\n442\t-   child included, and lower preserves both fields; a runnable\n443\t-   child refuses export; a restored child continues from its saved\n444\t-   state without rerunning initialization or replaying the link\n445\t-   request. Version 1 adds: phase and parent required, and a\n446\t-   child's carried ids checked in the root's context. The two\n447\t-   version-0 defects of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 are fixed with\n448\t-   version-0 tests. Stage: D.\n449\t+1. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the active carrier gates version 3, not legacy mixed-version bodies.\n450\t+   Version gate (7.2.1). Fixtures omit the version, give a different\n451\t+   version or a float `3.0`, or corrupt a child task's profile. Carrier\n452\t+   gate/structure validation refuses before attach, proposals or restore;\n453\t+   an exclusive composition refuses a nil-custody fork. There is no\n454\t+   legacy fallback. Stage: C inspector fixtures (7a), D lower (7a/7b).\n455\t+2. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: custody now occupies one restricted root slot.\n456\t+   Custody header (7.2.1). Fixtures drop required exclusive keys,\n457\t+   use explicit fork policy, nil occurrence, self-origin, extra keys\n458\t+   (including current lease, epoch or holder), or non-origin keys on\n459\t+   a halted root. Every child custody slot must be nil. The five-key\n460\t+   grammar and by-kind rules refuse `:yin.k/undecodable` with a path.\n461\t+   Fork is nil for all kinds. Stage: C inspector, D lift/lower (7a).\n462\t+3. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: counter/issue restoration now runs through rows and fenced recovery.\n463\t+   Sequence state (7.7.8). Assign-before-append, one increment,\n464\t+   retention across full/unknown effect/suspended, exact operation-counter\n465\t+   restoration and children drawing from the root remain required.\n466\t+   Fenced recovery also restores the carried id counter exactly and\n467\t+   ordered issue stamps from local recovery state; ordinary resume adopts\n468\t+   max(local, carried) for id-counter. Stage: D (7a/7b), C durable input\n469\t+   replay, E crash/regrant (7d).\n470\t+4. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the rows custody grammar checks carried ids across the tree.\n471\t+   Carried ids (7.4.3, 7.7.8). Put, FFI-request and link-request ids\n472\t+   cross park/pack/lower unchanged and retry through fencing. Fixtures\n473\t+   cover sequences at/above counter, duplicate ids, missing origin,\n474\t+   self-occurrence and ids anywhere in a fork tree; next has no op-id slot.\n475\t+   Protection/enrollment mismatch in either direction refuses before\n476\t+   attach. An enrolled retained write without an id refuses lift as\n477\t+   `:unprotected-pending`. Stage: D (7a/7b/7c); C retains authoritative\n478\t+   checkpoint membership and ancestry checks. General pending correlation\n479\t+   agreement remains open in continuation §11 Q4.\n480\t+5. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: install validation now enforces parent and manifest/image agreement.\n481\t+   Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3). Parked-id names a\n482\t+   carried explicit record and waits restore in order; an install pending\n483\t+   names a carried record. Both wire phases running/parked are admitted,\n484\t+   but lower requires child kind blocked. Parked/halted children refuse;\n485\t+   running phase is synthetic, with narrowing left open in continuation\n486\t+   §11 Q1/Q10. Lower checks response status, manifest module/exports and\n487\t+   image admission, non-nil parent equals response link id, and the\n488\t+   manifest-named derivation record's hash/tree-input/image-A-output.\n489\t+   Failures refuse before attach; restored children do not initialize or\n490\t+   replay the link request. Composition-known content binding remains\n491\t+   linker's fetch/admission responsibility. Stage: D (7b/7c).\n492\t 6. Envelope (7.7.8). Fixtures drop each key, change the dispatch\n493\t    value, give a wrong author, an unbound lease, and a foreign id,\n494\t    and nest an envelope-shaped program value. Assert each defective\n495\t@@ -3308,10 +3327,20 @@ Clauses, with the stage that owes the evidence\n496\t    no successor is eligible, and admission answers `:suspended`\n497\t    before tenure. The values 2^52 and -1 are refused on the bytes.\n498\t    Stage: C for the epoch, D for the sequence.\n499\t-10. Composition (linker 14.2.4). The crash, partition, reclaim, and\n500\t-    successor-completion contracts run through the wired handoff\n501\t-    composition on both host matrices, with crash cuts around\n502\t-    completion and around result delivery. Stage: E.\n503\t+10. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7d ports Stage E to the canonical kernel and distinguishes host evidence.\n504\t+    Composition (linker 14.2.4). Crash, partition, reclaim and completion\n505\t+    contracts run through semantic-register source/receiver compositions\n506\t+    using packs. Recovery format 2 is stored before the whole pack, both\n507\t+    fenced before publication. Pack addresses name offers, checkpoints\n508\t+    and report results; the baseline includes the structural root.\n509\t+    Equal-baseline alternatives retain that root in another row order;\n510\t+    an id-counter variant changes root and is `:variant-conflict`.\n511\t+    Canonical emit sorts rows, while decode admits unsorted alternatives.\n512\t+    The 7d report records local JVM acceptance and a clean JVM/JVM\n513\t+    process gate; it does not close Node/Dart or killed four-pair JVM/Node\n514\t+    acceptance. Stage: E (7d), pending the orchestrator's host matrix.\n515\t+    The REPL custody gap is accepted until phase 8; backend fragment\n516\t+    checks (continuation §4.6) remain separate from Stage E.\n517\t \n518\t Canonical fixtures prove grammar parity. They do not prove\n519\t atomicity, attribution, or recovery; those need the durable\n520\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24e59ef2-1e11-40bc-bf74-9b03c65bd1ca","uuid":"f3b22dd6-e342-4f6c-83c5-1b8f4cb28589","timestamp":"2026-10-13T18:46:04.824Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/.claude/projects/-Users-sto-workspace-datomworld-srvm-p7e/24e59ef2-1e11-40bc-bf74-9b03c65bd1ca/tool-results/by52rfidu.txt","content":"diff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\nindex 5156a4d7..c9631bc4 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\n@@ -380,19 +380,20 @@ site is an explicit park and an `:act` at an `:ffi-call` site or a\n Resource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cursor cells lower into\n the receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\n M4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\n-snapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\n-reachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n-§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\n-contract permits), the response verbatim as structural data, and the\n-child as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. \"Verified\"\n-means verified by the **receiver**, never trusted because it decodes:\n+snapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table carries every\n+parked record (continuation §2.5). An `:install` row carries the complete\n+entry of\n+§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, non-nil parent link id, the\n+response verbatim as structural data, and the\n+child as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. The intended \"verified\" contract\n+requires receiver verification, never trust merely because it decodes:\n the response's manifest names the module the row is keyed by, its link\n id equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\n is delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\n contract and the child's profile agree (`yin.vm.linker.md` §5, §9;\n amendment §2). A failure is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row.\n-**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §5.2 B1, §7.2):** the\n-merged lower (`rows/check-install!`) verifies, before any attachment:\n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7b enforces install link and manifest/image agreement at lower.\n+The merged lower (`rows/check-install!`) verifies, before any attachment:\n the phase is one of `#{:running :parked}` (`:install-phase`); the\n response is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` whose\n `:yin.module/name` is the row's module and whose `:yin.module/exports`\n@@ -401,9 +402,16 @@ receiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`); and the carried\n child task's kind is `:blocked` (`:install-child`), the only kind the\n engine retains at a safepoint (a halted or parked child is linked in the\n sweep it halts in, so phase `:running` is a wire phase the codec accepts\n-but the engine never exposes at a safepoint). The link-id-equals-parent\n-and image-address-equals-manifest checks of the paragraph above are\n-**not** implemented (continuation §11 Q3).\n+but the engine never exposes at a safepoint). Lower also requires\n+non-nil `parent` equal to response `:yin.link/id` (`:install-link-id`).\n+The response's `:derivation` record must hash to the manifest's\n+`[:yin.module/derivations :yin.semantic/code]` address; its\n+`:yin.ledger/input` must equal `:yin.module/tree` and its output must\n+hash-identify the image's `:value` vector (`:install-image`). These are\n+response self-consistency checks; the linker's source-index, fetch and\n+admission path binds code and trees to composition-known addresses.\n+A remains the only code identity and the Universal AST is never lost.\n+No new resolver or independent claimed-image-address check is added.\n \n ### 5.5 The root\n \n@@ -440,25 +448,27 @@ neither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\n never collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\n ordered waits.\n \n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner chose nil forks and phase 7 restricts custody keys.\n `custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n §7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n \n | role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n |---|---|---|---|\n | root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n-| root | blocked / parked | fork | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` |\n+| root | blocked / parked | fork | nil |\n | root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n | root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n | child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n \n-The explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\n-empty-header fork representation: a fork root proposes nothing and is\n-never upgraded to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n-`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\n-unchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n+Fork custody is nil in every row; it proposes nothing and is never\n+upgraded to custody. An exclusive-only receiver refuses a fork as\n+`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. The slot admits only `:yin.k/policy`,\n+`:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/arbitration`, `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and\n+`:yin.k/origin`. No current lease, epoch or holder enters a body;\n+origin's predecessor lease is lineage evidence.\n \n The root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\n-address**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n+semantic identity**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n are inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\n plain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\n keeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\n@@ -687,22 +697,23 @@ reference per cursor cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n \n ## 7. Equality and the content address\n \n-The transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\n-the root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cursor cell\n-id through the references, and to every cursor cell's contents through the\n-census. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\n-and under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\n-renaming of cursor-cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\n-the row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\n-the pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\n-Jing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\n-supplies the order within each body and the numbering of cursor cells. Jing is\n-asked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n-\n-Occurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\n-the body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\n-occurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\n-occurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: content stores verify the whole pack's bytes, not the root row's hash.\n+The transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. The root id\n+commits to reachable content and cursor-cell contents through the census;\n+equal root ids express semantic equality under §6's canonical numbering.\n+The **pack address** is BLAKE3 of the canonical CBOR bytes of the whole\n+pack. It is the content-store key, ledger `:yin.k/id`, grant checkpoint,\n+offer variant id and report `:yin.k/result`; it differs from the root id.\n+The baseline records the root as `:yin.k/root`.\n+\n+`emit` sorts `:yin.k/rows` by the string of each row id, so canonical\n+emission yields one pack for one root on each host. `decode` still\n+accepts other row orders. Reversing rows therefore gives another pack\n+address with the same root and baseline; re-emitting an id-counter change\n+changes the root and is `:variant-conflict` (7a D2). Occurrence and origin\n+remain inside the root's hash. The holder stores the whole pack as one\n+object, after recovery format 2 and before journaling the fence; it does\n+not store rows separately.\n \n ## 8. Validation on lower\n \n@@ -800,15 +811,15 @@ one attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n     operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n     never adds missing header keys.\n \n-**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §7.2).** The merged\n-`rows/lower-task` runs steps 8-10 as four stages, **each over the whole\n-task tree before the next, and all before the first attachment** (step\n-11): (a) stores: no reserved name keys the root store or a module store\n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phase 7 runs custody once after three per-tree stages.\n+`rows/lower-task` runs stores, pendings/install records and dependencies\n+**each over the whole task tree before the next**, then custody once for\n+the tree, all before the first attachment (step 11): (a) stores: no reserved name keys the root store or a module store\n (`:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`); (b) pendings and install\n records: role, every `:install` pending names a carried install, kind\n consistency, parked id, every install record as §5.4 as amended\n (`:role :incomplete-install :kind :parked-id :install-phase\n-:install-response :install-child`); (c) dependencies: `requires`'\n+:install-response :install-link-id :install-image :install-child`); (c) dependencies: `requires`'\n `:yin.k/segments` equals `code`'s key set (`:requires`), every segment\n has a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry, every `store-of` of this task's\n closures and environments names one of **this task's** module-store\n@@ -816,10 +827,15 @@ rows (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/stores …}`), every\n vector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`:segment`), every\n closure row names a closure tuple of its vector with the same binders\n (`:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`), every `:prim` is the receiver's\n-(`:yin.k/unsatisfied`); (d) custody: nil only (`:yin.k/unsupported\n-:custody`). Of step 8's text, the op-id rules and the correlation\n-agreement are not implemented (fork-only custody has no `next-op-seq`;\n-continuation §11 Q4); of step 9's, the census is not recomputed from the\n+(`:yin.k/unsatisfied`); (d) custody: `custody-baseline` validates the\n+slot and op-ids across the tree, refusing fork ids, missing origin,\n+self-occurrence, out-of-range sequences and duplicates. A fork under\n+`:exclusive` refuses; halted exclusive needs no grant; blocked/parked\n+exclusive checks grant evidence, the pack checkpoint, binding, tenure,\n+input prefix and protection/enrollment in both directions before attach.\n+`:fenced` skips grant/protection and restores exporting state (§9).\n+The broader correlation agreement remains unimplemented\n+(continuation §11 Q4); of step 9's, the census is not recomputed from the\n rows (continuation §11 Q2), and `requires` beyond segments, trees and\n primitives (modules, streams, cursor profiles) is not evaluated on\n lower; free names are discharged at lift only (continuation §7.1).\n@@ -856,12 +872,32 @@ environment, the walk (which fixes the segments, contexts and module\n stores), code, the per-context dependency closure over live code, tree\n coverage from the composition's `:source-index`, frame validation of\n the assembled task, then canonical numbering and emit; the slice is the\n-whole root store and every parked record; custody is nil. The nested carrier's value table\n+whole root store and every parked record.\n+\n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phase 7 adds the header and custody checks after frame validation.\n+`:header` nil means fork; otherwise enrollment remains local and the root\n+slot is written by kind after exhaustion/protection checks. The lift then\n+runs `custody-baseline` for the tree and emits sorted rows (§7). It can\n+serve streams during the walk before a custody refusal; the holder\n+retains successful answers for retries. The nested carrier's value table\n (`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cursor cell\n table becomes the census.\n \n **Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n \n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: format-2 recovery uses an administrative fenced lower.\n+`rows/lower-task {:fenced true}` runs the carrier checks and custody\n+grammar before attach, skips grant/protection, then gates every task\n+`:exporting`. Waits and all parked records move to\n+`:yin.k/recovery-waits` and `:yin.k/recovery-parked`, outside the scheduler;\n+the id counter is the carried value exactly and\n+`:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` retains resource-key/cursor-cell associations.\n+Child spawning disables stream construction and initializes nothing.\n+The recovery reader checks its local header and `:yin.k/state` against\n+the pack before lower, extracts the export record, and requires identical\n+re-encoded pack bytes. It restores no tenure or runnable gate and refuses\n+abort after restart. Ordinary resume still adopts `max(local, carried)`.\n+\n The laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n \n - **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\ndiff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\nindex 4b575812..c15025bb 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\n@@ -1920,10 +1920,11 @@ kind `:reason-mismatch`, with the holding task's path; the lift\n refuses the same holds on its own behalf. A task is not at a liftable\n safepoint while it holds any of these.\n \n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the holder now exports rows with a local header and source index.\n The lift's custody input is one **header** argument -- supplied to\n-`holder.export/prepare [machine record serve! header]`, stored in the\n-export record as `:header`, and passed to `export-task` through its\n-`:header` option -- `nil` or:\n+`holder.export/prepare [machine record serve! header source-index]`,\n+stored as `:header`, and passed to `rows/lift-task` through its `:header`\n+option -- `nil` or:\n \n     {:yin.k/occurrence  O\n      :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity i\n@@ -1934,39 +1935,51 @@ export record as `:header`, and passed to `export-task` through its\n      :yin.k/next-op-seq n\n      :yin.k/enrolled    #{stream-identity ...}}\n \n-A `nil` header lifts today's version-0 fork; a header lifts version 1,\n-exclusive, in the canonical codec, with `:yin.k/policy\n-:yin.k/exclusive` added by the lift. The occurrence is minted once by\n-the driver and persisted before prepare is called; `:yin.k/enrolled`\n-is the composition's declaration from the ledger reader; it is never\n-encoded into the UCF body, but the header stays in the retained export\n-record. The live record can contain authentic closures and is not itself\n+A nil header lifts a version-3 fork with nil custody; a header lifts an\n+exclusive root with custody by kind (continuation §2.6). The driver mints\n+and persists the occurrence before prepare. Enrollment stays local,\n+never in the pack. The source index is the composition/linker's\n+`{A #{tree-address}}`, supplied by `compose/open!` as a function and\n+read by the driver; holder configs cannot override it. Code and trees\n+load through the linker's source-index, fetch and admission; A is the\n+only code identity and the Universal AST remains retrievable.\n+\n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: export recovery is format 2, one pack plus holder-local state.\n+The live export record can contain authentic closures and is not itself\n a canonical data object. `holder.export/freeze [machine prepared-record]`\n-encodes a separate content-addressed export-recovery object, tagged\n-`:yin.k/export-recovery` with format version 1, using the handoff value/cursor-cell\n-codec. It returns recovery bytes/address beside the exact handoff body\n-bytes/address; both are stored durably before the journal's fenced event.\n-Recovery retains the local enrolled declaration, prior gate, ordered waits,\n-parked continuations, issue stamps, descriptors and dependency closure.\n-The enrolled declaration still never travels in a published handoff body.\n-\n-`holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` validates the recovery object and its\n-embedded body before administrative attachment. It creates authentic\n-fresh-receiver-owned values and preserves resource aliases and distinct\n-cursor cells without program reads, cursor minting, writes, closes, initialization\n-or execution. Every task stays exporting, with waits outside scheduler\n-queues. The prior gate is retained only as data; recovery is neither grant\n-admission nor permission to abort back into execution. Runtime composition\n-functions and handles are the environment, not the canonical journal.\n-Incomplete preparations cannot manufacture a missing snapshot on restart.\n-Recovery also retains a complete codec snapshot when legacy fork bytes\n-omit transitive dependencies; the published version-0 bytes stay unchanged.\n-For version 1, the module-store fixed point is a correctness repair:\n-canonical ordering uses a separate scratch census and did not populate\n-the emitted dependency table. Bodies that previously omitted transitive\n-module stores now contain them and have different content addresses; this\n-is not a new handoff version or closure format. The transitive-store test\n-pins the old one-pass body and corrected body bytes by their addresses.\n+emits this separate content-addressed object:\n+\n+```clojure\n+{:yin.k/export-recovery true\n+ :yin.k/version 2\n+ :yin.k/pack-address a\n+ :yin.k/root B\n+ :yin.k/pack-bytes base64\n+ :yin.k/header h\n+ :yin.k/state {:gate g :origins n :issued n :issues [..]\n+               :children {module-name state}}}\n+```\n+\n+Here a is BLAKE3 of the published pack bytes and B is its structural\n+root id. The recovery object's address hashes its own canonical bytes.\n+The header is nil for fork and retains the local enrolled declaration for\n+exclusive; state retains each task's prior gate, origin/issued counters,\n+ordered wait issue stamps and recursive child metadata. The pack already\n+carries the whole store, every parked record and reachable module stores.\n+There is no second snapshot body or duplicate descriptor set.\n+\n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: fenced row lowering reconstructs data without admitting execution.\n+`holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` checks format, recovery address,\n+pack address/grammar, root, header/custody agreement and state/tree shape\n+before attachment. It calls `rows/lower-task` with `:fenced true`,\n+attaches once per stream identity, and creates fresh receiver-owned values\n+with cursor-cell aliases and distinct cursor cells preserved. Every task\n+stays exporting; waits and parked records are outside the scheduler and\n+move into the retained export record. Counters are exact; child spawning\n+initializes nothing and performs no program IO. Re-preparation uses\n+attached descriptors and requires byte-identical pack re-emission. No\n+tenure, custody permission or runnable gate is recovered; abort after\n+restart refuses. Composition functions and handles remain environment.\n \n The driver's exit persists recovery and body, journals the fence, attempts\n the stable successor offer, reports that exact successor/body, carries\n@@ -1992,24 +2005,20 @@ release opens with the next step, after the terminal record.\n Reopened holders do not regain tenure from persisted clock readings:\n they reconcile, release and re-enter candidacy, never resume execution.\n \n-Kind and\n-header agree at lift: blocked and parked roots carry occurrence,\n-arbitration, counter and policy, and origin when present; a halted\n-root carries origin only, and a header-bearing halt without an origin\n-is refused -- it is not automatically downgraded (a halt with no\n-origin at all is a version-0 result under a `nil` header); install\n-children carry no header. Before answering `:ok`, a version-1 root\n-lift runs `checkpoint/inspect` on its own bytes and address, then\n-`handoff/validate-body` on the decoded body; install children do not\n-independently undergo the root custody inspection. Any refusal\n-returns as the lift's refusal and nothing is published. A lift under a\n-header refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:op-seq-exhausted`, at the\n-2^52-1 counter of a blocked or parked root (a halted root ignores that\n-header value), kind `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`,\n-`:ffi-request` or `:link-request` entry, in the root or an install\n-child, with no operation id whose target identity is enrolled, and\n-`:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an operation id on an\n-unenrolled target.\n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: custody is checked on the abstract row task, not by re-reading legacy bytes.\n+Kind and header agree at lift: blocked/parked exclusive roots carry\n+policy, occurrence, arbitration, counter and optional origin; halted\n+exclusive roots carry origin only and a header-bearing halt without\n+origin refuses. Forks and all children carry nil. After walking and frame\n+validation, `rows/lift-task` checks exhaustion at 2^52-1 on a\n+blocked/parked exclusive root and protection across all retained\n+put/FFI-request/link-request waits, then runs `custody-baseline` before\n+emit. Enrolled writes without an id refuse `:unprotected-pending`; an id\n+on an unenrolled target refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream.\n+Fork ids refuse. Successful serves may precede these refusals; the holder\n+retains them for retry. The authority uses `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`,\n+whose baseline includes the root id. No current lease, epoch or holder\n+enters a body; origin carries predecessor lineage.\n \n Abort is permitted only before any possibly accepted offer attempt, or\n upon authoritative evidence that this occurrence has never been\n@@ -2890,7 +2899,12 @@ the review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\n   to row sets with equal root ids after the harness's specified\n   re-emission (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §5.5) from\n   the semantic-register kernel and from the stack and register kernels\n-  through their layouts, the stack kernel with its tail-completion seam;\n+  through their layouts, the stack kernel with its tail-completion seam.\n+  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner retains both kernels as backends on a fixed fragment.\n+  The stack and register kernels are backends of the semantic-register\n+  VM, checked on their documented fragment and required to refuse the\n+  unsupported remainder before attach (continuation design §4.6). They are retained\n+  after phase 8 without widening the fragment;\n   and the §7.3.4 image-equality assertion (the two AST projection lanes\n   and the direct vector load yield one image and address).\n - **Exporting-state integration.** The lift driver's exporting transition\n@@ -3052,9 +3066,11 @@ claim that all five blockers have closed.\n   item 12). Exercise distinct activation depths and captured environments\n   at one pc, a nonempty ready queue, a nonsafepoint pc, and a kept response\n   cursor shared by two waiters. Omit the portable cursor profile for the\n-  refusal case. Separately prepare the three engines intended to\n-  reconstruct canonical frames (the semantic-register kernel; the\n-  register kernel; the stack kernel after its tail-completion seam) with\n+  refusal case.\n+  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: cross-engine conformance covers the documented backend fragment.\n+  Separately prepare the canonical kernel and its two backends\n+  (semantic-register; register; stack after its tail-completion seam),\n+  within continuation design §4.6's fragment, with\n   a nontrivial call stack, a captured closure, and a blocked effect at\n   the handoff point.\n - Action and expected outcome: On each host, lift a parked task and lower\n@@ -3201,12 +3217,19 @@ they are not moved here and nothing below relies on them as custody\n evidence. The ownership row keeps its own contract; the rows below\n are the wire-level clauses that row's tests must also exercise.\n \n-- Invariant: A version-1 body, envelope, binding, and admission\n+**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phase 7 re-bases custody evidence on row packs without codec dispatch.\n+The clauses below now use version-3 packs and the inspector\n+`yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`. Stage C is authority/admission (7a),\n+Stage D is holder/composition (7b/7c), and Stage E is the scenario/process\n+matrix (7d). Direct versions 0/1/2 codec tests remain only until phase 8;\n+the holder and authority have no compatibility reader or version dispatch.\n+\n+- Invariant: A version-3 pack, envelope, binding, and admission\n   outcome mean the same on JVM, Node, and Dart, and every breach of\n   their grammar is a data outcome before a machine is restored or an\n   effect is committed.\n-- What M-next B claims: the grammar is published. It lands no code,\n-  passes no test, and closes no row. `handoff-version` is still 0.\n+- Historical M-next B claim: publication of grammar alone closed no\n+  runtime row. Phase-7 gates below require row-carrier evidence.\n - How each clause is tested on three hosts: by canonical byte\n   fixtures decoded on each host, so a rule about integer kind or key\n   presence is checked on the bytes and not on a host number type;\n@@ -3216,53 +3239,49 @@ are the wire-level clauses that row's tests must also exercise.\n Clauses, with the stage that owes the evidence\n (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3):\n \n-1. Version gate (7.2.1). Fixtures: version 2, absent, a float `1.0`,\n-   a version-0 child in a version-1 root. Assert\n-   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` with the found and supported versions,\n-   or `:yin.k/undecodable` for the mixed tree, with zero attach\n-   calls, zero proposals, and no machine: recursive validation has\n-   no side effect. The integral float is refused on Node as on the\n-   others. A version-0-only reader refuses a version-1 body. A\n-   version-0 body still lowers as a fork on a reader that speaks\n-   both; a composition requiring exclusive refuses it. Stage: D.\n-2. Custody header (7.2.1). Fixtures omit each required key, give a\n-   fork policy, a nil occurrence, an origin equal to the body's\n-   occurrence, and header keys other than the origin on a halted\n-   root. Root and embedded child are distinguished: any header key\n-   on a child, a halted child with an origin included, is refused,\n-   and a halted child without one validates. Assert\n-   `:yin.k/undecodable` with the path. Stage: D.\n-3. Sequence state (7.7.8). Assign-before-append, one increment,\n-   retention across `full`, unknown effect, and `:suspended`; the\n-   counter restored exactly; children drawing from the root.\n-   Stage: D for assignment and restoration; C for durable input\n-   replay; E for crash and regrant through the composition.\n-4. Carried ids (7.4.3, 7.7.8). An id crosses park, bytes, and lower\n-   unchanged on `:put`, `:ffi-request`, and `:link-request`, and the\n-   retry is fenced. Fixtures: an id on `:next`, a sequence at or\n-   above the counter, a duplicate id, an id in a body without an\n-   origin, an id naming the body's own occurrence. Assert\n-   `:yin.k/undecodable`. Protection mismatch in each direction is\n-   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream; a first exclusive export\n-   over an attempted write is `:unprotected-pending`. Stage: D for\n-   these structural checks. Stage: C for authoritative membership\n-   and ancestry: an ancestor id the granted checkpoint never\n-   carried is refused, a carried one is admitted, a child's carried\n-   id counts, and an orphan successor gives no ancestry.\n-5. Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3), both versions.\n-   A `:park` or `:call-effect` reason and an `:install` pending\n-   without its entry refuse at decode; a `:parked` body's frames\n-   restore in order; a response that fails verification and a phase\n-   outside `:running` and `:parked` refuse; each of the two phases\n-   crosses with each child kind `:blocked`, `:parked` and `:halted`,\n-   a `:running` phase over a blocked child and an explicitly parked\n-   child included, and lower preserves both fields; a runnable\n-   child refuses export; a restored child continues from its saved\n-   state without rerunning initialization or replaying the link\n-   request. Version 1 adds: phase and parent required, and a\n-   child's carried ids checked in the root's context. The two\n-   version-0 defects of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 are fixed with\n-   version-0 tests. Stage: D.\n+1. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the active carrier gates version 3, not legacy mixed-version bodies.\n+   Version gate (7.2.1). Fixtures omit the version, give a different\n+   version or a float `3.0`, or corrupt a child task's profile. Carrier\n+   gate/structure validation refuses before attach, proposals or restore;\n+   an exclusive composition refuses a nil-custody fork. There is no\n+   legacy fallback. Stage: C inspector fixtures (7a), D lower (7a/7b).\n+2. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: custody now occupies one restricted root slot.\n+   Custody header (7.2.1). Fixtures drop required exclusive keys,\n+   use explicit fork policy, nil occurrence, self-origin, extra keys\n+   (including current lease, epoch or holder), or non-origin keys on\n+   a halted root. Every child custody slot must be nil. The five-key\n+   grammar and by-kind rules refuse `:yin.k/undecodable` with a path.\n+   Fork is nil for all kinds. Stage: C inspector, D lift/lower (7a).\n+3. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: counter/issue restoration now runs through rows and fenced recovery.\n+   Sequence state (7.7.8). Assign-before-append, one increment,\n+   retention across full/unknown effect/suspended, exact operation-counter\n+   restoration and children drawing from the root remain required.\n+   Fenced recovery also restores the carried id counter exactly and\n+   ordered issue stamps from local recovery state; ordinary resume adopts\n+   max(local, carried) for id-counter. Stage: D (7a/7b), C durable input\n+   replay, E crash/regrant (7d).\n+4. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the rows custody grammar checks carried ids across the tree.\n+   Carried ids (7.4.3, 7.7.8). Put, FFI-request and link-request ids\n+   cross park/pack/lower unchanged and retry through fencing. Fixtures\n+   cover sequences at/above counter, duplicate ids, missing origin,\n+   self-occurrence and ids anywhere in a fork tree; next has no op-id slot.\n+   Protection/enrollment mismatch in either direction refuses before\n+   attach. An enrolled retained write without an id refuses lift as\n+   `:unprotected-pending`. Stage: D (7a/7b/7c); C retains authoritative\n+   checkpoint membership and ancestry checks. General pending correlation\n+   agreement remains open in continuation §11 Q4.\n+5. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: install validation now enforces parent and manifest/image agreement.\n+   Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3). Parked-id names a\n+   carried explicit record and waits restore in order; an install pending\n+   names a carried record. Both wire phases running/parked are admitted,\n+   but lower requires child kind blocked. Parked/halted children refuse;\n+   running phase is synthetic, with narrowing left open in continuation\n+   §11 Q1/Q10. Lower checks response status, manifest module/exports and\n+   image admission, non-nil parent equals response link id, and the\n+   manifest-named derivation record's hash/tree-input/image-A-output.\n+   Failures refuse before attach; restored children do not initialize or\n+   replay the link request. Composition-known content binding remains\n+   linker's fetch/admission responsibility. Stage: D (7b/7c).\n 6. Envelope (7.7.8). Fixtures drop each key, change the dispatch\n    value, give a wrong author, an unbound lease, and a foreign id,\n    and nest an envelope-shaped program value. Assert each defective\n@@ -3308,10 +3327,20 @@ Clauses, with the stage that owes the evidence\n    no successor is eligible, and admission answers `:suspended`\n    before tenure. The values 2^52 and -1 are refused on the bytes.\n    Stage: C for the epoch, D for the sequence.\n-10. Composition (linker 14.2.4). The crash, partition, reclaim, and\n-    successor-completion contracts run through the wired handoff\n-    composition on both host matrices, with crash cuts around\n-    completion and around result delivery. Stage: E.\n+10. **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7d ports Stage E to